---
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import { artworks } from '../data/artworks';
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
---

<BaseLayout title="Process — Axiom Atelier">
  <section class="practice-opening"><div><p class="practice-kicker">Earth · Mineral · Intervention</p><h1>Material &amp;<br />Process</h1><p>Creation is the revelation of form through essential subtraction.</p></div><Image src={artworks[1].cover} width={800} format="webp" alt="The mineral surface and displaced fissure of Tectonic Restraint" loading="eager" /></section>
  <section class="process">


    <p>Every work begins with an encounter with material.</p>

    <p>Earth gathered from the landscape. Mineral pigments formed through geological processes. Linen, wood, carbon, and the textures they carry. Each arrives with characteristics that existed long before I began working with it.</p>

    <p>I don't approach these materials with the belief that they need to become something else to possess beauty. My work begins with recognizing what is already there.</p>

    <p>In paintings such as <em>Terra</em> and <em>Tectonic Restraint</em>, earth and mineral pigments are accumulated, displaced, and reduced. Geometry emerges from disorder. A fracture interrupts an otherwise quiet surface. Sometimes the most important decision is not what to add, but what to leave undisturbed.</p>

    <p>This is the foundation of Latent Form Theory: creation is the revelation of form through essential subtraction.</p>

    <p>The process is deliberate, but not entirely predictable. Pigments settle according to their own physical properties. Moisture moves through a surface. Gravity, absorption, and time participate in the composition. My role is to recognize when these interactions reveal something worth preserving—and when further intervention would diminish it.</p>

    <p>The same principles extend to the furniture and objects I make. Whether working with quarter-sawn white oak, Peruvian walnut, or mineral pigment, I am interested in proportion, material honesty, and the discipline of removing what does not belong.</p>

    <p>Every piece is made by my own hand, from its earliest preparation through its final resolution.</p>

    <p>The objective is not simply to make something beautiful.</p>

    <p>It is to recognize the moment when nothing more is needed.</p>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .practice-opening { display:grid; grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr; gap:60px; align-items:center; padding:65px 0; border-bottom:1px solid #cfc6b8; }
  .practice-opening h1 { font-size:clamp(44px,6vw,80px); font-weight:400; line-height:1.03; letter-spacing:-.04em; margin:20px 0 30px; }
  .practice-opening img { width:100%; max-height:600px; object-fit:contain; }
  .practice-kicker { font:11px Arial,sans-serif; letter-spacing:.15em; text-transform:uppercase; }
  @media(max-width:760px) { .practice-opening { grid-template-columns:1fr; gap:25px; padding-top:40px; } }

  .process {
    padding: 80px 0;
    max-width: 700px;
  }

  h1 {
    font-size: 2.5rem;
    margin-bottom: 50px;
  }

  p {
    font-size: 1.1rem;
    line-height: 1.7;
    margin-bottom: 30px;
    color: #2a2a2a;
  }
</style>